---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Services"
  description="A fixed thirty-day engagement to map and automate manual work, single fixed-scope builds, and embedded engineering — Cascadia Systems, Portland, Oregon"
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-topo section bg-gradient-to-b from-dark-bg via-dark-surface to-dark-bg">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="mb-6 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Services and pricing</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-text-secondary">
          Three engagements, all available now. Prices are fixed so the argument about scope
          happens once, on paper, instead of every month over hours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Diagnostic -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">The thirty-day engagement</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          <strong class="text-text-primary">$12,000. Half at the start, half at handover.</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            The first week goes to the people who do the work. Not only managers: the person who
            exports the report on Monday, the person who re-keys the form, the person who knows
            which spreadsheet is the real one. Five to eight conversations of about an hour each,
            plus read-only access to the systems they mention, so the process can be seen rather
            than imagined.
          </p>
          <p>
            By the end of the second week the client has a map of the five processes that cost
            the most hours, with the hours counted, the points where errors get in, and what each
            is costing. One is chosen together. Its scope goes on a single page that both sides
            sign, because the usual way these projects go wrong is the scope growing quietly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weeks three and four are the build, ending in production. Production means something
            specific here: the job runs on a schedule, logs what it did and what it could not do,
            fails in a way someone will notice, and comes with a written procedure for the day it
            breaks. Whoever will own it gets trained, usually in half an hour. The engagement
            closes with a twelve-month plan for the other four processes: which to automate next,
            which to buy software for, and which to leave alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            From the client: one person who can make a decision within two days, about six hours
            of staff time across the month, and the access described above. Write access is
            limited to the one process being automated and can be revoked at any time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-10">
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Ask about the thirty days</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Fixed-scope builds -->
  <section class="section bg-dark-surface">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">A single build</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          <strong class="text-text-primary">$5,000 to $15,000, quoted after a call.</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            For organizations that already know which process they want gone and don't need the
            thirty days to find it. The most common requests are intake forms that file
            themselves and their attachments where they belong, a nightly reconciliation that
            names every mismatch between two systems, a morning report that assembles itself from
            several sources, and scanned documents turned into records without retyping. Each
            ships the same way the thirty-day build does: scheduled, logged, documented, handed
            over.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Embedded -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">Embedded engineering</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          <strong class="text-text-primary">$25,000 to $35,000 a month, six months minimum, roughly half of one engineer's time.</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            An engineer inside the organization, on site or remote as the work requires, with
            ownership of the manual work as a whole rather than one process at a time. The first
            ninety days go to the worst process. After that, one more into production every month
            or six weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every thirty days, a written agreement on what ships next. Every week, one page on
            what shipped, what is stuck, and what is being done about it. Everything documented as
            it is built, so the client's team could keep running what exists if the engagement
            ended tomorrow. The final month is handover.
          </p>
          <p>
            Embedded engagements are taken one at a time, so start dates depend on the current
            one. Ask, and the answer will be a date.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Data -->
  <section class="section bg-dark-surface">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-6 text-3xl font-bold">Data handling</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            A one-page data-handling sheet is provided before anything is signed. In short:
            client data stays in client systems; anything sensitive is processed on the client's
            machines or on hardware Cascadia Systems owns, not on a hosted model; where a hosted
            model is the right tool, it is a provider with a data-processing agreement, named in
            the scope; named accounts only; credentials rotated back at the end; working copies
            deleted and confirmed in writing. Nothing is trained on client data, and nothing built
            for one client is reused for another.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
        <h2 class="mb-6 text-3xl font-bold">Questions</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          The fastest way to find out whether this fits is a thirty-minute call. Email with the
          process in mind and a couple of times that work.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Contact</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
